  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When YNAB Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "YNAB remains the better option for households committed to strict zero-based budgeting who want to share a single subscription. The application requires users to allocate every dollar earned toward immediate expenses, true expenses, or savings goals. Because one YNAB subscription supports up to six people across separate accounts for $109 per year, a family of three or four pays roughly $18 to $36 per person annually, which is lower than buying individual subscriptions on other platforms.\n\nYNAB also provides a 34-day free trial that does not require entering a credit card upfront. This lets new users test transaction imports, category funding, and envelope reconciliation across an entire monthly pay cycle before committing money. For budgeters who want proactive cash allocation rather than passive expense reporting, YNAB provides a focused operational framework."
  },
  "verdict": "Goodbudget provides the only functional $0 free tier among these options, while Quicken Simplifi offers the lowest promotional annual cost at $47.88 per year. PocketGuard stands out with its $149.99 Lifetime plan, Tiller provides automatic imports directly into spreadsheets for $99 per year, and Monarch Money Core matches YNAB's monthly rate while charging $99.99 annually. YNAB remains the primary option for users seeking envelope budgeting with built-in multi-user sharing for up to six people at $109 per year.\n\nTo pick the best option for your household, identify whether you prefer envelope allocation or automated spending summaries, verify your device platform requirements, and compare total annual subscription costs in our table.",
